FIXED: /echo/middleware/compress.go (only file I changed).

BUG (missing empty-body guard in gzipResponseWriter.Write, was lines 111-116):
```go
func (w *gzipResponseWriter) Write(b []byte) (int, error) {
	if w.Header().Get(echo.HeaderContentType) == "" {
		w.Header().Set(echo.HeaderContentType, http.DetectContentType(b))
	}
	return w.Writer.Write(b)
}
```
Root cause: `gzip.Writer.Write([]byte{})` does NOT return early — it writes the 10-byte gzip header straight into the real ResponseWriter (verified with a standalone program: `n=0 err=<nil> len=10`). So a handler that writes an empty body (e.g. `c.Response().Write([]byte{})`) emitted 10 garbage bytes into the response body; then the deferred cleanup saw `res.Size == 0`, deleted `Content-Encoding: gzip` and reset `res.Writer`, leaving a corrupted, non-gzip body (and Content-Length deleted by WriteHeader). The empty case was also sniffing `Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8` for zero bytes.

AFTER (now lines 111-117):
```go
func (w *gzipResponseWriter) Write(b []byte) (int, error) {
	if len(b) == 0 {
		return 0, nil
	}
	if w.Header().Get(echo.HeaderContentType) == "" {
		w.Header().Set(echo.HeaderContentType, http.DetectContentType(b))
	}
	return w.Writer.Write(b)
}
```
